// prelude/mem — heap buffers and reference counting.
//
// Ownership (Rust Ch 4.1): every value has a single owner. `int buf[n]`
// owns n zero-filled slots, `string s[n]` n zero-filled bytes; `*int q =
// p` moves `p`. Calls borrow their arguments; `x.move()` shares any
// object, string, or array explicitly (same address, both stay valid),
// `str.copy(s)` makes an independent string. Owned values free
// themselves when they go out of scope (real munmap, verified by the
// frees test); `int` / `bool` are `Copy`.
//
//   strlen(s) -> int  byte length of a NUL-terminated string (borrows)
//   mem.retain(o) -> int  bump the refcount of a class object (o)
//   mem.release(o)       drop one reference (a plain refcount decrement)
//   mem.retainVal(o) -> int  null-guarded retain that returns o (the value
//                           stays usable in a typed slot of any class;
//                           `x.move()` is the same operation as a method)
//   mem.retainStr(v) -> int  retain a string held as a raw int slot (the
//                           collections' unsafe core; null-guarded)
//   mem.releaseStr(v)      release a string held as a raw int slot
//
// Buffers are indexed element-wise: `int buf[n]` slots load/store the
// int at byte offset i*8 (arrays skip their length header
// automatically), `byte buf[n]` / `string s[n]` the byte at offset i,
// and `short sbuf[n]` the 2-byte little-endian element at offset i*2.
// `len(b)` is the element count of any buffer.
int main() {
    string s = "memory";
    println(strlen(s));
    int buf[1];
    buf[0] = 7;
    println(buf[0]);
    return 0;
}
